**New evidence**
- [Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding Telephone Talks with President von der Leyen and President Zelenskyy](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00003.html) - Kishida said persistent diplomacy should lead to de-escalation and reaffirmed Japan’s support for Ukraine’s sovereignty and territorial integrity.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding Telephone Talks with President von der Leyen and President Zelenskyy","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-02-15","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00003.html","claim":"Kishida said persistent diplomatic efforts should pave the way for de-escalation and reaffirmed Japan's support for Ukraine's sovereignty and territorial integrity.","locator":"opening remarks on Ukraine and de-escalation","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO transcript of Kishida's in-period statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct early-February 2022 diplomacy before the full-scale invasion.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["This is diplomatic posture rather than a measurable settlement outcome."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- [Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding the Situation in Ukraine](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00008.html) - Kishida condemned Russia’s recognition of breakaway regions, called it a sovereignty violation, and said Japan would coordinate sanctions with the G7 and other partners.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding the Situation in Ukraine","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00008.html","claim":"Kishida condemned Russia's recognition of the breakaway regions as a violation of Ukraine's sovereignty and said Japan would coordinate sanctions with the G7 and the wider international community.","locator":"opening remarks, lines 19-21","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO transcript of Kishida's in-period statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate pre-invasion crisis response in February 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["Japan's main tools here were sanctions and diplomacy, not direct coercive leverage."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}

- [Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding his Telephone Talk with President Zelensky of Ukraine](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00016.html) - Kishida pledged $100 million in humanitarian assistance and said Japan stood with Ukraine’s citizens defending their sovereignty and territory.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by the Prime Minister regarding his Telephone Talk with President Zelensky of Ukraine","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202202/_00016.html","claim":"Kishida pledged 100 million dollars in humanitarian assistance and said Japan stood with Ukraine's citizens defending their sovereignty and territory.","locator":"opening statement on the Zelenskyy call and aid pledge","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO transcript of Kishida's call summary and aid announcement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate post-invasion humanitarian response in February 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["Humanitarian aid supports civilians but does not resolve the conflict itself."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- [Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202203/_00018.html) - Kishida announced stricter sanctions, ICC and UN inquiry support, and emergency humanitarian assistance for Ukraine.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-03-16","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202203/_00018.html","claim":"Kishida announced stricter sanctions against Russia, support for ICC and UN inquiries, and emergency humanitarian assistance for Ukraine.","locator":"opening statement and the enumerated three measures","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO transcript with detailed, dated policy steps.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy action after the invasion began.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person policy statement by Kishida.","contrary_evidence":["The package still centers on coercive pressure rather than direct mediation."],"lenses":["2B.2","2B.4","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- [Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202204/_00004.html) - Kishida said Russia’s conduct in Bucha and elsewhere amounted to war crimes and backed ICC and UN investigations.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-04-08","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202204/_00004.html","claim":"Kishida said the atrocities in Bucha and elsewhere were war crimes and backed ICC and UN investigations, including accelerated ICC contributions.","locator":"opening statement on further sanctions and accountability","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO transcript on a major accountability decision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct April 2022 response to battlefield atrocities.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["Accountability support does not substitute for ending the war."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.9"]}

- [Press Conference by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio Following the Japan-Australia-India-U.S. Quad Leaders' Meeting](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202205/_00013.html) - Kishida said the Quad agreed that the rule of law, sovereignty, and territorial integrity must hold in every region and also discussed North Korea’s missiles and denuclearization.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio Following the Japan-Australia-India-U.S. Quad Leaders' Meeting","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-05-24","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202205/_00013.html","claim":"Kishida said the Quad leaders agreed that the rule of law, sovereignty, and territorial integrity must hold in every region and discussed North Korea's missiles and complete denuclearization.","locator":"opening statement lines 20-23","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO transcript from a summit chaired by Kishida.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 summit diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as summit chair.","contrary_evidence":["The Quad is a deterrence-oriented format, not a peace settlement mechanism."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.10"]}

- [Video Message by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio at the Second Summit of the Crimea Platform](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202208/_00009.html) - Kishida reiterated support for Ukraine’s sovereignty including Crimea, rejected unilateral changes by force, and said Japan had already imposed strong sanctions and aid.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Video Message by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio at the Second Summit of the Crimea Platform","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-08-23","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202208/_00009.html","claim":"Kishida reiterated support for Ukraine's sovereignty including Crimea, rejected unilateral changes by force, condemned nuclear threats, and said Japan had imposed strong sanctions and support measures.","locator":"opening paragraphs of the video message","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO video transcript tied to a 2022 Ukraine diplomatic forum.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct August 2022 Ukraine diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["Solidarity statements do not prove battlefield effect or settlement progress."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- [Address by Prime Minister Kishida at the Seventy-Seventh Session of the United Nations General Assembly](https://www.mofa.go.jp/fp/unp_a/page3e_001242.html) - Kishida argued for rule of law over rule by force, condemned Russia’s aggression against Ukraine, and called for stronger UN, rule-of-law, and human-security efforts.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address by Prime Minister Kishida at the Seventy-Seventh Session of the United Nations General Assembly","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Japan","publication_date":"2022-09-20","url":"https://www.mofa.go.jp/fp/unp_a/page3e_001242.html","claim":"Kishida argued for rule of law over rule by force, condemned Russia's aggression against Ukraine, and called for stronger UN, rule-of-law, and human-security efforts.","locator":"lines 47-50, 56-57, 63-70, 90-105","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary diplomatic speech by Kishida at the UN General Assembly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct September 2022 multilateral diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person speech by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is broad multilateral rhetoric and not a negotiated settlement."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- [Japan-Ukraine Summit Telephone Meeting](https://www.mofa.go.jp/erp/c_see/ua/shin4e_000051.html) - Kishida told Zelenskyy Japan would keep strong sanctions and assistance, would not recognize the purported annexations, and would work toward peace and reconstruction.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan-Ukraine Summit Telephone Meeting","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Japan","publication_date":"2022-09-30","url":"https://www.mofa.go.jp/erp/c_see/ua/shin4e_000051.html","claim":"Kishida told Zelenskyy Japan would keep strong sanctions and assistance, would not recognize the purported annexations, and would work toward peace and reconstruction.","locator":"meeting overview items 1-3","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary MOFA record of the Kishida-Zelenskyy summit call.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct late-September 2022 crisis diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct attribution to Kishida in the official bilateral record.","contrary_evidence":["The commitment is diplomatic and supportive, not a direct mediation track."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- [Video Message by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio at the International Expert Conference on the Recovery, Reconstruction and Modernisation of Ukraine](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202210/_00015.html) - Kishida condemned attacks on civilians and nuclear threats, said reconstruction must be Ukrainian-owned and transparent, and pledged winterization and reconstruction support.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Video Message by Prime Minister KISHIDA Fumio at the International Expert Conference on the Recovery, Reconstruction and Modernisation of Ukraine","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202210/_00015.html","claim":"Kishida condemned attacks on civilians and nuclear threats, said reconstruction must be Ukrainian-owned and transparent, and pledged winterization and reconstruction support.","locator":"opening paragraphs, especially lines 27-35","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript of a 2022 Ukraine reconstruction forum speech.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct October 2022 reconstruction diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["Reconstruction diplomacy is downstream of the war, not conflict termination itself."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.4","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- [Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida regarding the Launch of Ballistic Missiles by North Korea](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202211/_00001.html) - Kishida ordered public information, damage checks, and analysis after repeated North Korean missile launches.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida regarding the Launch of Ballistic Missiles by North Korea","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202211/_00001.html","claim":"Kishida ordered public information, damage checks, and analysis after repeated North Korean missile launches.","locator":"opening statement lines 20-21","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript of Kishida's immediate crisis response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis management for missile incidents affecting regional security.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["This is crisis management, not a broader peace settlement."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}

- [Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida regarding the Adoption of the new National Security Strategy](https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202212/_00006.html) - Kishida said the 2022 strategy package was a response to Russian aggression and escalating regional armament, including counterstrike capabilities.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida regarding the Adoption of the new National Security Strategy","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202212/_00006.html","claim":"Kishida said the 2022 strategy package was a response to Russian aggression and rising regional armament, and he justified counterstrike capabilities as part of the new posture.","locator":"opening statement lines 29-32 and 43-44","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary PMO transcript of a major security-policy decision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct year-end 2022 security-policy shift.","ruler_attribution":"Direct first-person statement by Kishida as prime minister.","contrary_evidence":["This is the clearest evidence of a more militarized posture, even if framed as defense."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.8","2B.10"]}

- [Adoption of the new National Security Strategy](https://www.mofa.go.jp/press/release/press4e_003192.html) - The foreign minister said diplomatic capabilities were the first pillar of the new NSS and that Japan would use vigorous diplomacy to create a peaceful and stable international environment.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Adoption of the new National Security Strategy","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Japan","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.mofa.go.jp/press/release/press4e_003192.html","claim":"The foreign minister said diplomatic capabilities were the first pillar of the new NSS and that Japan would use vigorous diplomacy to create a peaceful and stable international environment.","locator":"statement items 3-4","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary MOFA release summarizing the Cabinet-approved strategy and diplomatic framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct year-end 2022 doctrine adoption.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Kishida's Cabinet and foreign policy apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["The same package also reinforces defense architecture rather than only diplomacy."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.8","2B.10"]}

- [National Defense Strategy](https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/JPSC/20221216.O2E.html) - The strategy text says counterstrike capabilities are a minimum necessary self-defense measure, not a preemptive strike policy, and ties them to alliance deterrence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National Defense Strategy","publisher":"The World and Japan Database","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/JPSC/20221216.O2E.html","claim":"The strategy text says counterstrike capabilities are a minimum necessary self-defense measure, not a preemptive strike policy, and ties them to alliance deterrence.","locator":"lines 204-210","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Scholarly database reproduction of the official strategy text with precise locators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct doctrinal language from December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Policy text adopted by Kishida's Cabinet; not a private speech.","contrary_evidence":["Even framed as self-defense, the text still marks a meaningful expansion in capability."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.8","2B.10"]}

- [Pacifist Japan unveils biggest military build-up since World War Two](https://www.yahoo.com/news/pacifist-japan-unveils-unprecedented-320-080314303.html) - Reuters reported that Japan’s December 2022 package was its biggest military build-up since World War Two and would buy missiles capable of striking China.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pacifist Japan unveils biggest military build-up since World War Two","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.yahoo.com/news/pacifist-japan-unveils-unprecedented-320-080314303.html","claim":"Reuters reported that Japan's December 2022 package was its biggest military build-up since World War Two and would buy missiles capable of striking China.","locator":"opening paragraphs of the Reuters report","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is strong independent monitoring; this is a republished wire report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct contemporaneous reporting on the December 2022 policy shift.","ruler_attribution":"The report attributes the build-up to Kishida's government and remarks.","contrary_evidence":["This is an external characterization and not Kishida's own framing."],"lenses":["2B.8","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across PMO, MOFA, MOD, UN-related materials, Diet-adjacent records, and Reuters/Reuters-republished reporting.
- I retained 13 new source-claim units from 13 URLs because they were the most direct 2022 Kishida-attributed records on Ukraine, Russia sanctions, reconstruction, North Korea missile crises, and the December security pivot.
- I rejected repeated mirrors of the same PMO/MOFA statement when the cleanest official version was already captured.
- I rejected later retrospective pages from 2023-2026 unless they only helped locate the original 2022 record; those later pages were not used as evidence for 2022 conduct.
- I rejected generic Japan security-background pages that did not add ruler-attributed 2022 conduct beyond the official strategy text already retained.
- I rejected articles that restated the same Ukraine sanctions or condemnation line without adding a distinct action, locator, or attribution.
- I did not use the client matrix, and I did not re-fetch the local structured priors already supplied in the thread.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct evidence of behind-the-scenes mediation or ceasefire brokerage by Kishida in 2022 is still thin; the public record is strong on sanctions and solidarity, weaker on private peace brokering.
- Proxy-control evidence is mostly absent because Japan was not a belligerent and did not manage client militias or proxy forces in the way this rubric usually contemplates.
- The end-of-year defense buildup is well documented, but separating pure security necessity from domestic political signaling would benefit from cabinet minutes, Diet committee material, or a deeper secondary history.
- If you want, I can do one more pass focused only on 2B.5 and 2B.8 to tighten the proxy/control and military-mobilization picture.
